### What is the API Gateway pattern?

The API Gateway centralizes everything a client should not repeat. A route is a predicate plus filters. In Spring Cloud Gateway you route paths to services with filters for auth, rate limiting, and circuit breaking.

```java
package com.bank.gateway;

import org.springframework.cloud.gateway.route.RouteLocator;
import org.springframework.cloud.gateway.route.builder.RouteLocatorBuilder;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;

@Configuration
public class BankGatewayConfig {

    @Bean
    public RouteLocator bankRoutes(RouteLocatorBuilder builder) {
        return builder.routes()
            .route("payments", r -> r
                .path("/api/v1/payments/**")
                .filters(f -> f
                    .circuitBreaker(c -> c.setName("paymentCB"))
                    .requestRateLimiter(l -> l.setRateLimiter(redisRateLimiter())))
                .uri("lb://payment-service"))
            .route("accounts", r -> r
                .path("/api/v1/accounts/**")
                .uri("lb://account-service"))
            .route("dashboard", r -> r
                .path("/api/v1/dashboard")
                .uri("lb://aggregation-service"))
            .build();
    }
}
```

```text
Expected routing:
/api/v1/payments/PAY-001 -> payment-service (load balanced)
/api/v1/accounts/SAV2001 -> account-service (load balanced)
/api/v1/dashboard -> aggregation-service (fans out to multiple services)
```

The gateway can also aggregate: one client call to `/api/v1/dashboard` fans out to the account, payment, and card services and merges the responses, saving the mobile app three round trips.

### How does the gateway hide internal topology?

The gateway hides the internal topology so you can split or rename a service without changing clients. The mobile app only knows about the gateway's public URL.

```text
Before splitting loan from account:
  Client -> Gateway -> /api/v1/accounts/** -> account-service

After splitting:
  Client -> Gateway -> /api/v1/accounts/** -> account-service
  Client -> Gateway -> /api/v1/loans/**    -> loan-service (new)

Client code unchanged. Only gateway routes updated.
```

### What is the Circuit Breaker pattern?

The Circuit Breaker protects callers from a failing dependency. It counts outcomes over a sliding window and transitions between states.

```java
package com.bank.resilience;

import io.github.resilience4j.circuitbreaker.annotation.CircuitBreaker;
import io.github.resilience4j.bulkhead.annotation.Bulkhead;
import io.github.resilience4j.retry.annotation.Retry;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.logging.Logger;

@Service
public class AccountServiceClient {

    private static final Logger LOG = Logger.getLogger(AccountServiceClient.class.getName());

    @CircuitBreaker(name = "accountService", fallbackMethod = "debitFallback")
    @Retry(name = "accountService")
    @Bulkhead(name = "accountService")
    public void debit(String accountId, BigDecimal amount) {
        // HTTP call to account-service
        LOG.info("Debit of " + amount + " completed for " + accountId);
    }

    public void debitFallback(String accountId, BigDecimal amount, Throwable t) {
        LOG.warning("Circuit OPEN. Returning queued-for-retry for " + accountId);
        // Return a queued-for-retry response instead of failing the whole request
    }
}
```

```text
Circuit Breaker state machine:
CLOSED ──(failure rate > 50%)──> OPEN ──(wait 60s)──> HALF_OPEN
  ^                                                       │
  └──────────(trial calls succeed)────────────────────────┘
  HALF_OPEN ──(trial calls fail)──> OPEN
```

### How do you configure a Circuit Breaker?

You always pair the circuit breaker with a timeout so a slow call counts as a failure, and often a bulkhead so the dependency cannot consume all threads.

```yaml
# application.yml - Resilience4j configuration
resilience4j:
  circuitbreaker:
    instances:
      accountService:
        sliding-window-size: 10
        failure-rate-threshold: 50
        wait-duration-in-open-state: 60s
        permitted-number-of-calls-in-half-open-state: 3
  bulkhead:
    instances:
      accountService:
        max-concurrent-calls: 10
  retry:
    instances:
      accountService:
        max-attempts: 3
        wait-duration: 500ms
  timelimiter:
    instances:
      accountService:
        timeout-duration: 3s
```

```text
Behavior under load:
- Max 10 concurrent calls (bulkhead)
- Each call times out at 3s (timeout counts as failure)
- Up to 3 retries with 500ms wait
- If 50% of last 10 calls fail -> circuit opens for 60s
- In HALF_OPEN, 3 trial calls test recovery
```

### What is the Database-per-Service pattern?

The Payment Service owns the payments tables and the Account Service owns the accounts tables, with no shared schema and no cross-service joins.

```java
package com.bank.payment.repository;

import com.bank.payment.model.Payment;
import org.springframework.data.jpa.repository.JpaRepository;

// Payment Service's private repository.
// Only Payment Service code accesses this.
public interface PaymentRepository extends JpaRepository<Payment, String> {
    // No cross-service joins. No shared schema.
}
```

```java
package com.bank.payment.model;

import jakarta.persistence.Entity;
import jakarta.persistence.Id;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.time.Instant;

@Entity
public class Payment {

    @Id
    private String paymentId;
    private String fromAccountId;  // ID reference, not a JPA relationship
    private String toAccountId;    // ID reference, not a JPA relationship
    private BigDecimal amount;
    private String status;
    private Instant createdAt;

    // The Payment Service stores fromAccountId as a plain string.
    // It never JOINs with the Account Service's tables.
    // If it needs the holder's name, it calls the Account Service
    // or consumes account-events into a local read model.
}
```

```text
Data access pattern:
Payment Service needs account holder name?
  Option 1: Call account-service REST API (synchronous)
  Option 2: Consume account-events from Kafka into local read model (async)
  NEVER: SELECT from account-service's database directly
```

### How do these three patterns reinforce each other?

These three patterns interlock. Database-per-Service forces services to talk over the network. Those network calls are exactly what the Circuit Breaker protects. And the API Gateway is the one place where you enforce authentication and rate limiting for all of them.

```text
  Client
    │
    ▼
  ┌─────────────────────────────┐
  │  API Gateway                │  <-- Auth, rate limit
  │  (Spring Cloud Gateway)     │
  └─────────────┬───────────────┘
                │
       ┌────────┴────────┐
       ▼                 ▼
  ┌──────────┐    ┌──────────────┐
  │ Payment  │    │   Account    │
  │ Service  │──>│   Service    │  <-- Circuit Breaker
  └────┬─────┘    └──────┬───────┘
       │                 │
  ┌────┴─────┐    ┌──────┴───────┐
  │Payment DB│    │ Account DB   │  <-- DB-per-Service
  └──────────┘    └──────────────┘
```

### What is the tradeoff of Database-per-Service?

The cost is that you lose cross-service ACID transactions and must use the Saga pattern and eventual consistency.

```java
package com.bank.saga;

import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.logging.Logger;

@Service
public class TransferSaga {

    private static final Logger LOG = Logger.getLogger(TransferSaga.class.getName());
    private final AccountServiceClient accountClient;

    public TransferSaga(AccountServiceClient accountClient) {
        this.accountClient = accountClient;
    }

    public void execute(String fromId, String toId, BigDecimal amount) {
        try {
            accountClient.debit(fromId, amount);  // Local tx in Account Service
            accountClient.credit(toId, amount);   // Local tx in Account Service
            LOG.info("Transfer completed: " + fromId + " -> " + toId);
        } catch (Exception e) {
            accountClient.credit(fromId, amount);  // Compensating action
            LOG.warning("Transfer compensated: re-credited " + fromId);
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output (credit fails):
WARNING: Transfer compensated: re-credited SAV2001
Rajesh's money is returned. Eventual consistency is maintained.
```

### What are common mistakes with these patterns?

The most common mistake is using Database-per-Service in name only while still sharing a database.

```java
package com.bank.antipattern;

// WRONG: Payment Service queries Account Service's database directly.
public class WrongPaymentService {
    // SELECT holder_name FROM accounts WHERE id = 'SAV2001'
    // This creates hidden coupling. Schema changes in Account Service
    // break Payment Service without warning.
}

// CORRECT: Payment Service calls Account Service API or reads from local copy.
public class CorrectPaymentService {
    // accountClient.getAccountHolder("SAV2001");
    // OR consume account-events into a local read-model table.
}
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

The senior-level point is that these three patterns reinforce each other and are the minimum viable set for a production microservices architecture. Size is a distraction: the real test is whether a service can be deployed alone, and these three patterns are what make that possible.
